{
 "id": "roots-place",
 "part": "Δ",
 "el": "Ρίζες της θέσης και της κατοχής",
 "title": "Root families: putting, placing, standing, having, staying, taking, giving",
 "level": "B1–C1",
 "intro": [
  {
   "p": "Seven roots for where things are and who holds them. These are the most productive in all of Greek: with the prepositional prefixes they make hundreds of abstract nouns (**υπόθεση**, **κατάσταση**, **σχέση**, **παράδοση**) that dominate formal texts."
  },
  {
   "tip": "Each family starts from a root, often in two or three shapes (the present stem, the aorist stem, the ε/ο switch). The Greek verb under the heading is the everyday word that still carries the root; where the everyday word is different (παίρνω for λαμβάνω, γυρίζω for στρέφω) both are given."
  }
 ],
